          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for chronic insomnia?",
          "answer": "Both sleep medicine and internal medicine guidelines recommend Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Insomnia (CBT-I) as the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia in adults, prioritizing it over medication. (Source: guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line)\n\nCBT-I is recommended because its effects tend to last even after the treatment concludes, unlike the effects of medication, which generally do not. Additionally, CBT-I helps avoid the potential harms associated with long-term use of sleep medications. (Source: guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line)\n\nThe therapy typically involves four to eight sessions and includes components such as:\n*   **Stimulus control:** This involves using the bed exclusively for sleep and leaving the bed if unable to sleep, to prevent the bed from becoming associated with wakefulness.\n*   **Sleep restriction:** This technique limits the time spent in bed to align more closely with actual sleep time, gradually increasing it as sleep becomes more consistent. (Source: guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
